# Browser-harvest probe artifacts -- NEVER COMMITTED.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS. The publication-harvest probes (Bloomberg, Seeking Alpha)
# drive a real browser and write their evidence into the tree they run in:
# screenshots of a sign-in flow, a bot wall, and an AUTHENTICATED session, plus
# DOM dumps of modals. Measured 2026-09-14: 97 such files sat here untracked and
# UNIGNORED, 11 of them named LOGIN / LOGGED_IN / modal_dom.
#
# Nothing leaked -- they were never committed. The hazard was one keystroke wide:
# a `git add -A` from this directory's parent would have put captures of a
# logged-in session into a shared repository.
#
# THE POINT WORTH KEEPING: a screenshot's sensitivity is INVISIBLE TO EVERY
# TEXT-BASED GATE. The ASCII gate, the secret scanners and the comp-rail all read
# text; none of them can see what is rendered in a PNG. So the control has to be
# an ignore rule, because no scanner downstream will catch it.
#
# Probe evidence is kept ON DISK deliberately -- it is how a harvest run is
# audited afterwards. It is simply never version-controlled.

*.png
*.html

# If a capture must be preserved in the repo, it is reviewed for visible session
# state FIRST and then force-added by name -- never by a bulk add.
